WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.933
Tim: Thank you Heather MacDonald. Thank you
for heroically persevering. Before introducing

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.133
our next speaker, I would like to remind all
of you to visit the Hillsdale College literature

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.866
table. There you will find several college
publications including Imprimis. We're nearing

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
2.9 million monthly subscribers and growing
quickly.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.933
You will also find information about the many
on and off campus lecture programs sponsored

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
by the college as well as our online education
courses. We're getting ready to release 2

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.666
new courses: A Proper Understanding of K12
Education: Theory In Practice and the other

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.666
is The Federalist Papers. You can sign up
for these courses and watch archived courses

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
for free. Just visit our website and you can
see courses on economics, politics, literature

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.066
and history among other topics.
I would also invite you to pick up a brochure

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
for the next Hillsdale College cruise, which
will sail this summer from Lisbon to London

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
aboard the Six Star Crystal Serenity. Don't
delay. We'll be packing up the literature

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
right after lunch. The more you take, the
less we have to take home with us.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.046
I had the privilege of meeting our next speaker
and his lovely wife Penny when I came to Claremont,

00:00:06.046 --> 00:00:12.576
California, in August of 1994 fresh from Hillsdale
College and ready to study for my PhD in political

00:00:12.576 --> 00:00:18.076
science. At the time, he was the president
of the Claremont Institute. He didn't offer

00:00:18.076 --> 00:00:25.076
any formal classes back then, as he does now,
but he did a lot of teaching anyway informally,

00:00:25.296 --> 00:00:30.606
so I learned a lot from him about statesmanship
and political philosophy, whether in informal

00:00:30.606 --> 00:00:36.017
conversations or listening to his speeches.
Back then, when he was the president of the

00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:39.796
institute, I used to think he was a college
professor trapped in a think tank president's

00:00:39.796 --> 00:00:46.426
body. Now I think he's a college professor
trapped in a college president's body. Of

00:00:46.426 --> 00:00:51.006
course, all joking aside, those 2 things go
together at Hillsdale, as they used to in

00:00:51.006 --> 00:00:56.197
most other ... Well, at all colleges and universities
until recent times.

00:00:56.197 --> 00:01:02.127
Anyway, our students are fortunate to have
him as a professor and a president, I count

00:01:02.127 --> 00:01:07.607
it as a privilege to have been able to work
with him now for 15 years plus, I think, or

00:01:07.607 --> 00:01:12.897
almost 15 years at Hillsdale College. He will
be speaking today on the topic Conservatism

00:01:12.897 --> 00:01:17.366
and Constitutionalism. Please welcome the
twelfth president of Hillsdale College, Dr.

00:01:17.366 --> 00:01:24.366
Larry P. Arnn.
Larry: That Tim is tall, isn't he? Thank you

00:01:41.437 --> 00:01:48.437
Tim. Thank you for being here. Wasn't Heather
heroic? I'm thinking about my maturity this

00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:59.817
week because it's present, but also I've watched
Larry Kudlow become a gentle human being.

00:02:02.497 --> 00:02:09.497
He's always been brilliant and always been
good, but now he's nice. I've watched Heather

00:02:12.707 --> 00:02:19.707
grow up and become a power. That was really
great. I thank them both.

00:02:20.357 --> 00:02:24.916
Doug Jeffrey always picks these topics, and
they always call up the speakers and tell

00:02:24.916 --> 00:02:29.726
them what to say. They never do that with
me. They just assign me a topic. I don't know

00:02:29.726 --> 00:02:34.336
if they figure I'll say whatever I please
or if they figure we've worked together so

00:02:34.336 --> 00:02:39.636
long and studied together so long, I'll know
exactly what they mean. I always have confidence

00:02:39.636 --> 00:02:45.737
about that, except last night Doug broke into
verse, so I don't know what's going on with

00:02:45.737 --> 00:02:47.636
the boy and I don't know what he wants me
to do.

00:02:47.636 --> 00:02:53.616
I'll tell you what I will do, I'm to talk
about Conservatism and Constitutionalism,

00:02:53.616 --> 00:02:58.366
and that's a problem. There's something interesting
about that. The constitution is one of the

00:02:58.366 --> 00:03:02.806
greatest liberal documents in human history.
The greatest living ... The great constitution.

00:03:02.806 --> 00:03:06.757
The greatest written constitution by far.
There isn't anything close to it to last so

00:03:06.757 --> 00:03:11.907
long, do so much.
The principles behind it, expressed in the

00:03:11.907 --> 00:03:16.277
Declaration of Independence are fundamentally
important, and they are the most beautiful

00:03:16.277 --> 00:03:23.277
political principles ever written. They're
not conservative in their nature. The word

00:03:25.807 --> 00:03:32.807
liberal comes from the word free. Why aren't
we liberals? I'm going to try to explain that.

00:03:35.097 --> 00:03:42.097
I'm going to explain it mainly by talking
about 2 people. They are teachers of mine

00:03:43.217 --> 00:03:50.217
and they have both died in the last 6 weeks.
One of them is Harry Jaffa, a student of Aristotle

00:03:50.347 --> 00:03:57.347
and Abraham Lincoln, Winston Churchill, Thomas
Aquinas, Shakespeare, a great teacher of political

00:04:00.037 --> 00:04:05.347
philosophy. He wrote Barry Goldwater's "Extremism
in the defense of liberty" speech.

00:04:05.347 --> 00:04:12.136
The other is Sir Martin Gilbert. Sir Martin
Gilbert is ... Was - he died just 2 weeks

00:04:12.136 --> 00:04:19.136
ago. He was the official biographer of Winston
Churchill. He wrote 88 serious books of history.

00:04:19.647 --> 00:04:25.927
These 2 men have a very great deal to do with
this question of the disposition of conservatives

00:04:25.927 --> 00:04:32.787
to constitutions and to the American Constitution
because one of them did a very great act of

00:04:32.787 --> 00:04:39.787
recovery, which I will describe to you. The
other one did the same.

00:04:40.217 --> 00:04:47.097
The first one a recovery of the thinking and
meaning of the American Revolution and the

00:04:47.097 --> 00:04:53.507
second one of the life and career of Winston
Churchill. I've been studying Winston Churchill

00:04:53.507 --> 00:04:58.706
for a long time.
These 2 men, I should tell you, they were

00:04:58.706 --> 00:05:04.247
incredibly sophisticated, influential in my
life and sophisticated people. We have the

00:05:04.247 --> 00:05:11.247
papers of both of them at Hillsdale College
now. I met my wife, the aforementioned Penny

00:05:12.247 --> 00:05:19.247
back there, the mother of our children, working
in the home of Martin Gilbert a long time

00:05:20.057 --> 00:05:26.456
ago in Oxford.
Since Harry Jaffa introduced me to Martin

00:05:26.456 --> 00:05:33.456
Gilbert and Martin Gilbert introduced me to
Penny, they can both take credit for my marriage

00:05:34.386 --> 00:05:41.386
and felt that I owed them everything and would
say so. I'd acknowledge the fact. Mr. Jaffa,

00:05:41.486 --> 00:05:48.486
who was more personally assertive than Martin
Gilbert, would also take credit for my children.

00:05:49.427 --> 00:05:53.666
I would say, "You know, but she did that."
They would say, "Yeah, but how did you meet

00:05:53.666 --> 00:06:00.546
her?"
This is a significant thing and both of them

00:06:00.546 --> 00:06:07.546
are crucial to understanding where we are
in the world today in my opinion. Let me say

00:06:07.927 --> 00:06:14.927
where that is. Heather just described a crisis.
By the way, she's a very intelligent young

00:06:21.037 --> 00:06:26.027
woman, and she just gets better with every
passing year, which means that her character

00:06:26.027 --> 00:06:31.557
is good. I've known her a long time and watched
her talk for a long time. I never saw her

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:36.557
do better than she did today.
I expect in a year or 2, when I see her again,

00:06:36.557 --> 00:06:43.557
she'll do better yet. She's that sort. She
hinted at some element of despair at the end.

00:06:45.386 --> 00:06:52.386
These problems are very deep. They are what
I said last night. The principle of self rule,

00:06:56.476 --> 00:07:02.856
the principle that all men are created equal
focuses on this simple thing. Some of us are

00:07:02.856 --> 00:07:07.427
smarter than others and some of us are taller
than others and some of us are richer than

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:14.427
others, but we've all got this one thing and
it makes us human. We can talk.

00:07:16.687 --> 00:07:23.507
Studying with Professor Jaffa 400 years ago,
it was an electric experience because I studied

00:07:23.507 --> 00:07:30.507
with a student of his by accident in undergraduate
school. I'm not a lawyer. I love to say I

00:07:31.997 --> 00:07:38.997
didn't do that. I decided to get an education.
Professor Jaffa was not a really great teacher.

00:07:41.297 --> 00:07:47.237
He was disordered and self-centered.
The first time I met him summer of 74, I was

00:07:47.237 --> 00:07:54.237
given the great honor of going to pick him
up to take him to go to the first class. Peter

00:07:56.596 --> 00:08:02.237
Schramm, my brother-in-law, took me. There,
we walk in the thing, and I'm going to meet

00:08:02.237 --> 00:08:07.937
this great man, author of the Goldwater speech,
author of the best book about Abraham Lincoln,

00:08:07.937 --> 00:08:14.937
and he appears in his boxer shorts.
He can't remember who I am and he can't remember

00:08:16.607 --> 00:08:23.607
what the class is we're going to have. I think
to myself, "Golly gosh, what have I done?"

00:08:24.487 --> 00:08:28.707
I'm just a kid too, so I didn't expect red
carpets or anything. I just thought maybe

00:08:28.707 --> 00:08:35.707
he would recognize that I had corresponded
with him. I can remember the first thing he

00:08:37.406 --> 00:08:44.406
said in class and it bears on this point,
what it is that makes us human.

00:08:46.557 --> 00:08:51.396
He started the first class, and he hadn't
prepared in any way, except by his whole life.

00:08:51.396 --> 00:08:57.487
The class was on Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics,
which I teach now and which I read. I can't

00:08:57.487 --> 00:09:02.757
tell you how many times I've read it, but
I think it might be once a year since 1974.

00:09:02.757 --> 00:09:09.757
I know it pretty well.
Here's how he starts the class. He says, "When

00:09:11.947 --> 00:09:18.187
people get old like me, they make a list of
the 100 greatest books, but that's foolish

00:09:18.187 --> 00:09:24.896
because nobody lives long enough to master
100 great books. Can't be done. I have a list

00:09:24.896 --> 00:09:27.906
of the 3 greatest books." What follows is
a little cryptic. I've thought about it for

00:09:27.906 --> 00:09:34.906
years. I remember every word of it, and it
was what? 41 years ago.

00:09:37.336 --> 00:09:44.336
He said, "Aristotle's politics would require
some translation to be fully relevant to modern

00:09:45.057 --> 00:09:52.057
times. It sounds like he's excluding it."
He said, "The Bible is its own kind of thing."

00:09:52.346 --> 00:09:59.346
Maybe he was was excluding it. Plato's Republic
would be on any such list.

00:10:02.656 --> 00:10:09.656
He said the word Shakespeare. It hung in the
air for a while and he said, "Socrates said

00:10:11.586 --> 00:10:16.727
that a poet would arise that could write both
tragedy and comedy. Seems to include Shakespeare."

00:10:16.727 --> 00:10:22.216
Certainly, I know he did. Then he held up
the Ethics and he said, "This is a perfect

00:10:22.216 --> 00:10:29.216
book, and we are going to read this book.
We are going to find out what we are."

00:10:31.327 --> 00:10:36.387
Not everything in class was as good as that
with him because, as I say, he was disordered.

00:10:36.387 --> 00:10:43.387
He was just brilliant, but it would happen
2 or 3 times a term out of 15 that he would

00:10:43.586 --> 00:10:48.346
start explaining something fundamental to
you. My favorite thing - I heard him explain

00:10:48.346 --> 00:10:55.346
it many times - was what it is human beings
can do that makes them human. Can talk is

00:10:56.057 --> 00:11:03.057
the short answer. It's actually true that
because we can talk, it means we can ... How

00:11:04.137 --> 00:11:10.586
do you give a name to a thing?
How do you know it's a bottle because there's

00:11:10.586 --> 00:11:17.586
many bottles. They look all different, but
once you can recognize that that's a bottle,

00:11:19.086 --> 00:11:25.577
you can use the term bottle. Professor Jaffa
referred to this as the miracle of the common

00:11:25.577 --> 00:11:31.156
noun. You can never explain how you can do
it. Man, man, man, woman, woman, woman, they're

00:11:31.156 --> 00:11:38.156
all different. Yet, in an instant, you can
see what they are and gives them a name. Once

00:11:41.346 --> 00:11:47.146
you can give them a name, you can communicate
in a radically different way than any other

00:11:47.146 --> 00:11:51.387
being.
Aristotle says animals ... It's in the politics.

00:11:51.387 --> 00:11:58.387
He says, "Animals use their voices to indicate
pleasure and pain, but human beings use theirs

00:12:00.987 --> 00:12:07.987
to describe the just and the unjust, the advantageous
and the disadvantageous." Do you see how we

00:12:08.417 --> 00:12:15.417
do that about the just and the unjust? If
this is a bottle, then it's in a category

00:12:16.047 --> 00:12:22.107
of things, and it would need to be treated
according to this category.

00:12:22.107 --> 00:12:27.107
That phenomenon is vitally important because
you can eat a pig, but you can't eat a human

00:12:27.107 --> 00:12:34.107
being. That's because you can know the difference
between them. They have different natures,

00:12:34.146 --> 00:12:39.887
and they're indicated to be treated in different
ways. That's how justice arise. You learn

00:12:39.887 --> 00:12:46.167
this from Professor Jaffa.
He loved the Declaration of Independence because

00:12:46.167 --> 00:12:52.757
he said that your rights are written in that
capacity. They don't matter how smart you

00:12:52.757 --> 00:12:59.086
are or how much money you've got or who your
parents were or what color you are. If you

00:12:59.086 --> 00:13:05.576
can do that thing, then you are entitled to
all the rights of the human being. From that

00:13:05.576 --> 00:13:12.576
fact grows these tremendous things.
I grew up in Arkansas, and my dad was a school

00:13:15.037 --> 00:13:22.037
teacher and a learned man, only one in his
generation or before to go to college. I didn't

00:13:22.716 --> 00:13:29.716
think he was wise until I became mature because
I was too stupid, but now I remember what

00:13:29.896 --> 00:13:36.317
I learned from him. I didn't know I was learning.
Heather said boys need their daddies and their

00:13:36.317 --> 00:13:39.727
mothers. I didn't remember ... I didn't know
I was learning these things from him, and

00:13:39.727 --> 00:13:44.987
I was good at learning. I got really good
grades.

00:13:44.987 --> 00:13:51.987
I know how I was raised. I was raised to think
that it didn't matter anything except that

00:13:52.526 --> 00:13:58.026
I was a human being and was supposed to be
a good one. Everything hinged on whether I

00:13:58.026 --> 00:14:05.026
did that or not. I was born to do whatever
I could do. I always thought I could do a

00:14:06.766 --> 00:14:13.766
lot. We didn't have any money. We lived in
a little town in Arkansas. There were creditors

00:14:14.826 --> 00:14:18.276
and stuff. I just thought that's how everybody
lived.

00:14:18.276 --> 00:14:24.766
I had a friend that ... I still have him.
His son is graduating from our college. He's

00:14:24.766 --> 00:14:29.136
a medical doctor now. I thought he was rich.
I know what it is to be rich. I know what

00:14:29.136 --> 00:14:32.857
it is to be rich now. I always thought, by
the way, I was going to be rich. I am now

00:14:32.857 --> 00:14:37.727
what I thought I was going to be. I just know
now it's not rich. There probably are some

00:14:37.727 --> 00:14:43.336
rich people in this room, but I happen to
know most of you are not really rich. You're

00:14:43.336 --> 00:14:50.336
just generous. Thank you.
That stuff. Grew up in America, you learn

00:14:52.697 --> 00:14:59.697
to love that stuff. That's what's being taken
away. Harry Jaffa is the one who started the

00:15:01.716 --> 00:15:08.716
movement to make the modern conservative movement
understand the greatness of all that stuff

00:15:10.646 --> 00:15:15.197
because in the beginning, the conservative
movement which started in the late 40s and

00:15:15.197 --> 00:15:22.197
50s, it was all about German economists ... They're
good too. I love them ... And about all kinds

00:15:22.857 --> 00:15:28.096
of things, but it wasn't on about the Declaration
of Independence and the constitution, the

00:15:28.096 --> 00:15:34.047
substance of American citizenship.
That was because a lot of those people suspected

00:15:34.047 --> 00:15:40.547
that those things were not good things because
the account on the right when I started graduate

00:15:40.547 --> 00:15:47.537
school of the American founding was that it
was a low solid thing. It was as good as you

00:15:47.537 --> 00:15:54.537
could get in the modern world. Harry Jaffa
is the one who thought that through and saw

00:15:54.627 --> 00:16:01.047
not.
I'll tell you how he did it. I happened to

00:16:01.047 --> 00:16:08.047
be there. The turn came in the summer of 1976.
I was a student there, and I really wanted

00:16:08.917 --> 00:16:12.957
to learn from him. The other students did
too, but I was so radical about it, that I

00:16:12.957 --> 00:16:19.957
took up bicycle racing. I've ridden a bicycle
25,000 miles. Why? Because he did it and you

00:16:20.497 --> 00:16:24.197
could be around him all the time, and you
could get him to answer your flipping questions,

00:16:24.197 --> 00:16:29.747
which in class sometimes you couldn't get
him to do.

00:16:29.747 --> 00:16:36.257
I would go over every summer. He was writing
an attack on a conservative man Irving Kristol

00:16:36.257 --> 00:16:40.167
because Irving Kristol had written this thing
about how the American Revolution is low,

00:16:40.167 --> 00:16:46.337
but solid. Professor Jaffa, for the first
time he'd been reading Lincoln. He'd started

00:16:46.337 --> 00:16:53.337
reading it for himself, and what did he find?
Did you ever hear this story of George Washington

00:16:53.477 --> 00:17:00.477
at the Battle of Monmouth, at the Battle of
Princeton? It's like a story about Achilles

00:17:02.907 --> 00:17:09.907
except maybe better.
Marching his horse at the British at Princeton,

00:17:10.317 --> 00:17:16.507
not knowing whether anybody was coming with
him, riding through American troops being

00:17:16.507 --> 00:17:23.507
routed. If they had been routed, it would
have destroyed the advantage of the victory

00:17:23.707 --> 00:17:30.407
at Trenton. Then we could have closed the
first year of the Revolutionary War without

00:17:30.407 --> 00:17:37.407
anything looking like victory. Had to win.
Then he heard. He took Trenton, just miracle.

00:17:38.137 --> 00:17:42.687
They were drunk and asleep.
He had to preserve it because he thought,

00:17:42.687 --> 00:17:45.637
"We've got to win something." He'd said to
somebody that morning, George Washington,

00:17:45.637 --> 00:17:52.357
"We'll be dead by nightfall anyway." "We've
got to go ahead. We're late," said the guy

00:17:52.357 --> 00:17:58.507
... What's his name? Clover. A New Englander,
who is good at boats, he said, "General, we're

00:17:58.507 --> 00:18:03.767
late. You said we had to be there by dawn."
Washington said to him, "Go ahead. It doesn't

00:18:03.767 --> 00:18:07.537
matter. We'll be dead by nightfall if we don't
win today anyway."

00:18:07.537 --> 00:18:12.287
Then the next day they had to go up to Princeton.
He gets there, and they're running, and he

00:18:12.287 --> 00:18:17.687
just walks through them. He doesn't say anything
to them. He was a very big man, very beautiful

00:18:17.687 --> 00:18:24.687
man. Fitzwilliam, his adjutant, records the
story. He gets close to the British. There's

00:18:28.057 --> 00:18:35.057
an exchange of fire. Fitzwilliam says he covers
up his face with his hat. He couldn't bear

00:18:35.367 --> 00:18:42.367
to look upon the destroyed George Washington.
George Washington was a tremendous horseman.

00:18:42.917 --> 00:18:48.857
When the smoke cleared, Fitzwilliam looks,
and he sees that he's sitting on his horse

00:18:48.857 --> 00:18:54.197
just as he was and making the same steady
progress that he was. There's been a volley.

00:18:54.197 --> 00:18:57.877
He's been right in the middle of it, and he's
still going. The Americans have formed up

00:18:57.877 --> 00:19:04.447
behind him without him saying anything to
them. The British run away. That man was picked

00:19:04.447 --> 00:19:10.107
to be the President of the United States.
You couldn't call the constitutional convention

00:19:10.107 --> 00:19:15.057
... Never mind that Alexander Hamilton and
James Madison are alive, Thomas Jefferson

00:19:15.057 --> 00:19:22.057
is alive, and they're all working on it, Jefferson
from Paris. Never mind that. If Washington

00:19:22.747 --> 00:19:29.747
won't go, that man won't go, it can't be done.
The reason is he is a picture of the moral

00:19:31.537 --> 00:19:38.417
virtues, one of the greatest in all of history.
That means then all of a sudden ... Harry

00:19:38.417 --> 00:19:43.997
Jaffa more than anybody else is the fellow
who discovered this. I happened to be there

00:19:43.997 --> 00:19:50.997
when he did it. What a privilege, the excitement
of it. He'd delivered his paper, an attack

00:19:51.697 --> 00:19:56.267
on a bunch of conservatives who had written
that America is corrupt and low and you can't

00:19:56.267 --> 00:20:00.817
expect much from it, but by golly we like
it because it would be a lot worse if it was

00:20:00.817 --> 00:20:06.237
communist. That was their argument.
I watched 400 people at the American Political

00:20:06.237 --> 00:20:11.867
Science Association in 1976 come into the
room for this argument. I watched my teacher

00:20:11.867 --> 00:20:18.867
wipe the floor with them. I learned my belligerence
at the hands of a master.

00:20:26.247 --> 00:20:33.137
I got a Rotary fellowship, and Professor Jaffa
had met this man, Martin Gilbert, young then,

00:20:33.137 --> 00:20:39.117
the official biographer of Winston Churchill.
The great Churchill Papers were in Oxford

00:20:39.117 --> 00:20:43.397
and only to be used by him, which I didn't
know at the time, but soon learned had the

00:20:43.397 --> 00:20:48.487
whole world of strands up in arms against
Martin Gilbert because, A, he was hogging

00:20:48.487 --> 00:20:54.327
these papers and, B, he didn't write his books
about them and their books. He wrote the book

00:20:54.327 --> 00:21:01.327
out of the original source documents.
I met him in the coffee shop. Professor Jaffa

00:21:06.017 --> 00:21:13.017
had done him a favor so he had to meet me.
I had this fancy fellowship to go study in

00:21:13.187 --> 00:21:20.077
England. I can remember every word that was
spoken. He was like a little ... He didn't

00:21:20.077 --> 00:21:23.817
look like ... I thought he would look like
Neville Chamberlain. You should see a picture

00:21:23.817 --> 00:21:30.817
of him. He's a little Jewish guy, very bright,
very powerful, tremendous conversationalist.

00:21:31.027 --> 00:21:38.027
I meet him, and I'm young and stupid, so what
I should have said was, "I want to work on

00:21:38.847 --> 00:21:44.197
the Churchill Papers," but I didn't think
that I did. I thought, "I'm going to write

00:21:44.197 --> 00:21:49.917
a doctoral thesis about Churchill's political
thought. That's to be found best in published

00:21:49.917 --> 00:21:56.917
sources. I don't need it." In a great kindness,
he said, "Can I help you?"

00:22:00.377 --> 00:22:06.767
We had met, by the way, in a library called
the Wiener Library up close to Regent Park.

00:22:06.767 --> 00:22:11.537
That's a library where some little Jewish
ladies keep a bunch of Nazi books. He was

00:22:11.537 --> 00:22:18.537
looking at one when I first walked up beside
him. It was open, and it was a children's

00:22:19.447 --> 00:22:26.447
book, a school book. Heather said, "What are
we teaching the young?" Nazism is a branch

00:22:33.667 --> 00:22:40.667
of this historicism that has America in its
grip. Communism is a branch. The Confederacy

00:22:43.357 --> 00:22:50.357
and its idea that slavery is good is a branch.
He showed me this book. This book was about

00:22:51.457 --> 00:22:56.107
some little German kids with blonde hair going
into a butcher's shop. It's a little school

00:22:56.107 --> 00:23:02.937
book, little kid book, colored pictures. The
butcher - it was a Jew - looked like Bluto

00:23:02.937 --> 00:23:09.937
in the Popeye thing with stubble. Martin Gilbert
said ... I said, "Oh hi, what are you looking

00:23:11.947 --> 00:23:18.947
at?", whispering. He showed me, and he said,
"Notice these sides of pork ... The Jewish

00:23:20.557 --> 00:23:27.557
butcher is selling pork.
The story ... By the way, this book was edited

00:23:27.877 --> 00:23:31.607
by the agency run by Julius Streicher, who
is one of the worst people ever to live, one

00:23:31.607 --> 00:23:38.607
of the senior Nazis. He said, "Notice that
the ... The story was about the butcher killing

00:23:40.107 --> 00:23:45.027
the Christian children when they came in,
the Aryan children, when they came in. He

00:23:45.027 --> 00:23:50.767
said, "Notice that the skin color of the sides
of pork hanging are the same as the skin color

00:23:50.767 --> 00:23:57.767
of the Jewish butcher."
We see that. He shows me that. I'm learning

00:23:57.947 --> 00:24:02.147
that he's interested in that stuff. Then we
walk down to the London School of Economics

00:24:02.147 --> 00:24:09.147
and we have coffee. He says, "Can I help you?"
I said, "No, I don't really need anything."

00:24:10.427 --> 00:24:13.557
It's really stupid.
It emerged while I was talking to him that

00:24:13.557 --> 00:24:19.777
I'd been reading his books. I could talk about
them. I'll give you a clue if you ever meet

00:24:19.777 --> 00:24:25.337
authors or have a teacher, nothing better
than knowing their work. They really like

00:24:25.337 --> 00:24:30.557
that. It's the reason the faculty is happy
at Hillsdale College.

00:24:30.557 --> 00:24:37.557
Students pay attention and learn and hold
them in respect. It's an idyllic life. I had

00:24:37.607 --> 00:24:44.037
that for him, but I didn't really want to
get digging in those papers very much. I said,

00:24:44.037 --> 00:24:49.497
"But I'm here in London, and I will help you
in any way. Call me any time. I will look

00:24:49.497 --> 00:24:52.777
up things. I will do whatever you want me
to do."

00:24:52.777 --> 00:24:59.777
He looked at me puzzled, like I was a Martian.
He said, "Would you like a job?" I said, "Yes."

00:25:02.817 --> 00:25:09.217
He said, "When could you start?" It was Friday.
I said Monday. I didn't say, "What would I

00:25:09.217 --> 00:25:14.137
do?" I didn't say, "How much would you pay
me?" He said, "Good. See you Monday." He left.

00:25:14.137 --> 00:25:21.137
I had to chase him and say, "By the way, where
should I go?" He said, "The Map House, Harcourt

00:25:21.967 --> 00:25:26.057
Hill, Oxford."
"Oxford?" He said, "Yeah, the cab driver will

00:25:26.057 --> 00:25:31.397
know where it is." "Oxford? Should I live
in Oxford?" He said, "That would probably

00:25:31.397 --> 00:25:38.397
be better," so I moved over the weekend. The
lady who was managing the documents and the

00:25:39.317 --> 00:25:44.697
correspondence downstairs kept making grammatical
mistakes. She wanted a permanent job, and

00:25:44.697 --> 00:25:50.257
she didn't have one yet. He showed me one
of them, and said, "What's wrong with that?"

00:25:50.257 --> 00:25:57.237
I pointed out what it was. He said, "Everyone
knows that?" I said, "I don't know. I know

00:25:57.237 --> 00:26:02.367
it."
He said, "Do Americans know things like that?"

00:26:02.367 --> 00:26:08.297
She had written slimming the tide instead
of stemming the tide. I said, "Well, I don't

00:26:08.297 --> 00:26:15.297
know. I do." He said, "This lady will not
do," and he let her go. 5 or 6, 8 days later,

00:26:20.367 --> 00:26:26.567
he arrived there to get coffee. He said, "There's
a new lady tomorrow." I said, "Oh, a new lady?"

00:26:26.567 --> 00:26:32.637
He said, "Yes, she's very smart. 3 A levels
from Roedean." I said, "I'll look forward

00:26:32.637 --> 00:26:39.637
to meeting this new lady." What showed up
was not a lady. It was my wife.

00:26:41.887 --> 00:26:48.887
That's important because Churchill is important
because who is the greatest statesman? To

00:26:50.527 --> 00:26:57.527
live in this bureaucratic age where the engineering
project to overcome man has become advanced,

00:27:00.177 --> 00:27:07.177
Lincoln sees it in Colonel, in Seed, in the
Civil War. It's Churchill's theme all his

00:27:09.387 --> 00:27:16.387
life. Churchill is on a battlefield in 1898
in Sudan. The Madhi of Allah, who is the grandfather

00:27:23.457 --> 00:27:30.047
of the current ruler of Sudan, he founded
the first Islamic Republic as we know it today,

00:27:30.047 --> 00:27:37.047
this Madhi of Allah, in 1895. That's when
he did that.

00:27:37.227 --> 00:27:44.227
The current ruler of North Sudan and his cousin,
also a relation of this 19th century Madhi,

00:27:49.007 --> 00:27:56.007
are the 2 chief teachers of Osama bin Laden.
Winston Churchill is in a cavalry charge against

00:27:58.287 --> 00:28:03.057
these guys in 1898.
First of all, his life is the weirdest thing

00:28:03.057 --> 00:28:10.057
you'll ever meet. He was everywhere. He was
captured by the Boers in 1899, and the future

00:28:11.387 --> 00:28:16.727
president of the Boer Republic, Louis Botha,
was then the Region. Although he didn't capture

00:28:16.727 --> 00:28:22.477
Winston Churchill, Churchill decided that
he did and would never believe whatever evidence

00:28:22.477 --> 00:28:29.477
that rests to the contrast that anybody except
a famous man had actually captured him.

00:28:29.547 --> 00:28:35.527
Churchill didn't like these Sudanese, Dervishes
they were called, but on the other hand, he

00:28:35.527 --> 00:28:42.527
watched the modern weapons work on them, a
70,000 charge, 25,000 British. They lost 11,000

00:28:44.447 --> 00:28:51.447
killed in about an hour. The one they got
nearest the British line came within 150 yards.

00:28:54.047 --> 00:28:57.857
Churchill looked at that. You should read
the book called The River War, where he writes

00:28:57.857 --> 00:29:00.767
about it.
He's a young guy, very much on the make. He's

00:29:00.767 --> 00:29:07.767
demonstrating his heroism so he can elected
to parliament. He describes this battle. In

00:29:08.757 --> 00:29:15.597
one paragraph, he describes the charge of
the Dervishes, whom he doesn't like, but he

00:29:15.597 --> 00:29:22.567
says how brave they are and how the scene
for them is heat and bullets screaming and

00:29:22.567 --> 00:29:29.567
people screaming and horses screaming and
dust and smoke everywhere, charging through

00:29:29.917 --> 00:29:36.057
hell toward hell.
Then he goes back to the British, same paragraph,

00:29:36.057 --> 00:29:44.117
and he says, "The infantry fired steadily
and solidly because although they could hardly

00:29:44.117 --> 00:29:54.037
see the enemy, they still remained interested
in the work even if it became tedious." Soldiers

00:29:54.037 --> 00:30:02.467
carried ammunition and others carried water
jackets to keep the barrels from melting.

00:30:02.467 --> 00:30:10.287
Everybody moved like on a factory floor and
they killed 11,000 people.

00:30:10.287 --> 00:30:17.287
Churchill has the imagination to ask, "What
if 2 armies come together both able to do

00:30:17.287 --> 00:30:27.367
that?" In 1925, he writes of the phenomenon.
Mankind has never been in this position before.

00:30:27.377 --> 00:30:33.647
Without enjoying wiser guidance or improving
appreciably in virtue, he's got into his hands

00:30:33.647 --> 00:30:42.287
at last the means of his own destruction.
Death stands waiting at attention, waiting

00:30:42.317 --> 00:30:49.317
for a word from the frail being so long his
servant, not for once his master.

00:30:51.557 --> 00:30:54.827
We wouldn't be worried about these Islamic
terrorists. First of all, we shouldn't worry

00:30:54.827 --> 00:31:01.827
about over much. The ones who are hostile
and do aggressive things, we should kill them,

00:31:04.147 --> 00:31:11.147
but it's the possession of the worst kind
of weapons that makes it the most formidable

00:31:13.307 --> 00:31:17.677
because they'll probably use them if they
get them. Their societies could never invent

00:31:17.677 --> 00:31:23.537
those weapons, but once they're invented,
you might get them.

00:31:23.537 --> 00:31:30.537
Churchill thought that this phenomenon that
we're becoming so powerful that we could destroy

00:31:32.087 --> 00:31:39.087
ourselves. Churchill is 26 years old when
he first starts writing about this. As its

00:31:40.257 --> 00:31:46.917
mate, in peacetime politics, Churchill comes
into the British parliament at the same time

00:31:46.917 --> 00:31:52.577
as the Socialist Party comes into the British
parliament. He fights it all his life.

00:31:52.577 --> 00:31:59.577
He said in 1945, "The socialists who have
been in coalition with me to fight the Nazis

00:32:00.757 --> 00:32:06.267
could not realize their ultimate aims without
the use of a secret police, yes, I mean a

00:32:06.267 --> 00:32:13.267
Gestapo." He said that after the war when
they had been fighting these Nazis. Of course,

00:32:13.407 --> 00:32:18.197
historians are uniform in saying how stupid
that was and how it was a reckless moment

00:32:18.197 --> 00:32:23.127
on his part. The truth of the matter is he
said that over and over again all his life.

00:32:23.127 --> 00:32:28.837
He said it many times after that occasion
even though he lost that election because

00:32:28.837 --> 00:32:35.837
I can tell you what he feared.
I'll read it to you. "Not the Bolsheviks.

00:32:43.697 --> 00:32:47.337
There's not one concept in the philosophy
of the Russian Bolshevik which has not been

00:32:47.337 --> 00:32:53.047
realized, carried into action and enshrined
in immutable laws a million years ago by the

00:32:53.047 --> 00:33:00.047
white ant. Equally startling developments
lie already just beyond our fingertips in

00:33:05.966 --> 00:33:10.107
the breeding of human beings and the shaping
of human nature."

00:33:10.107 --> 00:33:17.107
If you watch the government acting now ... It's
been lawless for a long time. One of Churchill's

00:33:18.857 --> 00:33:25.857
favorite things to say was, "If you make 10,000
regulations, you destroy all respect for law."

00:33:26.267 --> 00:33:31.337
The new thing that's happened - it's emerged
just in the last 4 or 5 years - is that now

00:33:31.337 --> 00:33:37.587
things are done in a lawless way that affect
the relationship between the government and

00:33:37.587 --> 00:33:42.797
the people. That is to say that compromise
the ability of the people to influence the

00:33:42.797 --> 00:33:46.647
government. I think they're calculated that
way.

00:33:46.647 --> 00:33:51.847
Everything that tends to a control on human
speech, which is the central human gift, which

00:33:51.847 --> 00:33:57.317
is the theme of the Obama administration since
the day it started, and everything that affects

00:33:57.317 --> 00:34:04.317
who the voters are, so that the voters are
not picking who the voters are ... A government

00:34:05.787 --> 00:34:12.787
that is thinking like that, that maybe the
people won't vote for us, but these things

00:34:12.797 --> 00:34:19.767
are right to do anyway and we're going to
do them, that's the line that looks to me

00:34:19.767 --> 00:34:24.897
like that's been crossed.
Now look at the tools that are possible in

00:34:24.897 --> 00:34:31.897
the modern world. "Equally startling developments
lie already just beyond our fingertips," Churchill

00:34:33.037 --> 00:34:40.037
writes this in 1931, "In the breeding of human
beings and the shaping of human nature. It

00:34:40.327 --> 00:34:44.657
used to be said though you have taught the
dog more tricks, you cannot alter the breed

00:34:44.657 --> 00:34:51.657
of the dog, but this is no longer true. Human
beings may soon be made, not birthed, but

00:35:01.797 --> 00:35:05.497
grown under glass."
Churchill wrote this in the same year that

00:35:05.497 --> 00:35:11.227
Brave New World was published, but we know
because he wrote his wife about that he didn't

00:35:11.227 --> 00:35:18.227
read that book until 25 years later. He was
thinking, "We could interfere with the mental

00:35:21.577 --> 00:35:28.577
development of the beings, who are now being
born or rather made, in a way to fit them

00:35:31.397 --> 00:35:38.397
to jobs that we want them to do."
CS Lewis wrote that, "The power of man over

00:35:47.007 --> 00:35:54.007
nature can only be expressed as the power
of some men over others." Winston Churchill

00:35:54.617 --> 00:36:01.617
knew that too. What was his solution? What
he thought ... He left politics when he was

00:36:04.267 --> 00:36:10.397
80 years old, and he'd been in more than 50
years. He thought all these things at the

00:36:10.397 --> 00:36:17.397
start and at the end. That means that we have
a rich record of a genius about how to combat

00:36:19.217 --> 00:36:26.057
these kind of politics.
In war, what do you do? Don't fight unless

00:36:26.057 --> 00:36:33.057
you have to. Find a cheap way to win. Bring
into bear overwhelming force. Those are his

00:36:35.257 --> 00:36:42.257
lessons. Strategy is his word. In peace what
do you do? Limit the government, enforce the

00:36:48.127 --> 00:36:55.127
constitution, live under its limits. When
you provide the social safety net, which he

00:36:56.717 --> 00:37:02.067
favored and hoped to invent, people should
invest in it. It should be a property that

00:37:02.067 --> 00:37:09.067
is their own and that they can count.
In British times, they got stamp books from

00:37:09.447 --> 00:37:15.977
the Post Office under the scheme he created.
The taxpayer put in 2 pence and the employer

00:37:15.977 --> 00:37:21.707
and the employee put in 7 pence. Every 6 months,
they got their stamp book full of stamps and

00:37:21.707 --> 00:37:28.437
they knew how much money they had because
he said, "The way you take care of the poor

00:37:28.437 --> 00:37:35.437
is that they invest mainly in ways that will
give them a stake in the stability of the

00:37:35.937 --> 00:37:42.937
capitalist system. They will not have the
taint of the dole because most of the money

00:37:43.247 --> 00:37:50.197
is theirs."
"You can't have 10,000 laws," he would say.

00:37:50.197 --> 00:37:54.437
In America, the 2 changes to the American
Constitution are the bureaucratic state and

00:37:54.437 --> 00:38:00.697
the entitlement state. You get rid of the
entitlement state through savings accounts

00:38:00.697 --> 00:38:07.697
that people own. That's Paul Ryan proposes
to do. You get rid of the bureaucratic state

00:38:09.297 --> 00:38:16.137
by remembering that people are the best agents
to take care of themselves and there are plenty

00:38:16.137 --> 00:38:22.107
of laws already and old about what happens
when you do something that harms somebody

00:38:22.107 --> 00:38:28.557
else.
Churchill fought that war all his life. He's

00:38:28.557 --> 00:38:35.557
been dead 50 years and a few days, and it
still goes on. What would we think? Should

00:38:43.417 --> 00:38:50.417
we be like Larry Kudlow, an optimistic? Should
we be like Heather and pessimistic? Should

00:38:54.007 --> 00:39:01.007
we be like Churchill?
Do you want to know what his last words were

00:39:04.787 --> 00:39:09.227
in the House of Commons? In a speech about
nuclear warfare, "Never flinch, never weary,

00:39:09.227 --> 00:39:16.227
never despair." We have his lessons, and we're
alive today and the fight is on. There isn't

00:39:18.007 --> 00:39:25.007
any reason whatsoever that it cannot be won.
Thank you for helping.

00:39:34.997 --> 00:39:41.997
Tim: Thank you Dr. Arnn. We have time for
questions. We have students with microphones.

00:39:47.187 --> 00:39:52.607
Please wait for the microphones, and they
will bring the microphone to you.

00:39:52.607 --> 00:39:59.607
Steve: Thank you, Dr. Arnn. Steve Barney from
North Palm Beach, Florida. Thank you again

00:40:08.517 --> 00:40:13.117
for delivering the wit and wisdom that you
so often and generously give us. Thank you

00:40:13.117 --> 00:40:19.927
for bringing Heather MacDonald here this morning.
She spoke with truth and clarity. At the end

00:40:19.927 --> 00:40:26.927
of her talk, she pointedly talked about the
days when she was a student and she could

00:40:27.947 --> 00:40:34.947
read any author, irrespective of their pigmentation
or plumbing arrangements and said you can't

00:40:38.757 --> 00:40:45.627
do that today. I hoped you might have a comment
on that.

00:40:45.627 --> 00:40:52.627
Larry: Thank you. That's the great Steve Barney
of the Barney Charter School Initiative. He's

00:40:53.417 --> 00:40:58.717
very generous and very smart and a very good
principal, but his greatest single gift is

00:40:58.717 --> 00:41:05.707
that when he gets sentimental, he cries out
loud. Churchill loved to say of himself, "I'm

00:41:05.707 --> 00:41:12.277
a blubber baby."
The point is we are the subject of an engineering

00:41:12.277 --> 00:41:19.277
project now. Here's the way we're regulated.
That thing, I've been hearing it everywhere,

00:41:24.627 --> 00:41:31.257
and I think it may be true, that if you're
selling arms and ammunition, which there's

00:41:31.257 --> 00:41:38.097
a right in the constitution and in nature
to do, then it's hard to get a bank loan.

00:41:38.097 --> 00:41:42.257
The reason is the regulators are all over
the banks. All they've got to do is just raise

00:41:42.257 --> 00:41:48.317
an eyebrow.
Reading is like that too. The government is

00:41:48.317 --> 00:41:55.317
getting into the business of picking curricula.
The plan in the Common Core is that the courses

00:41:55.797 --> 00:42:02.797
will go each course from Plato to Amy Tan.
There's usefulness in education to survey

00:42:05.377 --> 00:42:12.377
courses that cover a lot of ground, although
that's a very great deal of ground, but there's

00:42:14.467 --> 00:42:18.717
also usefulness to study ...
Do you remember what I said about Professor

00:42:18.717 --> 00:42:25.717
Jaffa? He claims that he knew 3 books, and
he was a very learned man, very profound man.

00:42:31.117 --> 00:42:37.277
When are you going to pick something great
that is really great and teach them what it's

00:42:37.277 --> 00:42:44.277
like to know something great? That's banned.
We're not at the place now where some books

00:42:45.067 --> 00:42:52.067
are not allowed to be read, although the adoption
of text books in America at the K12 level

00:42:52.247 --> 00:42:59.247
is dominated by 3 states and they have politically
correct standards. If you can sell one of

00:42:59.467 --> 00:43:06.467
your text books to one of those 3 states,
you've made a lot of money and to all 3, and

00:43:06.517 --> 00:43:13.517
you're rolling. There's lots of controls now
in this centralized way of doing things. Whereas,

00:43:15.597 --> 00:43:22.597
by the way, an excellent translation of Plato's
Republic can be had on the internet for free,

00:43:24.997 --> 00:43:31.997
so why do you need all that?
It's been proved ... There's proof of it in

00:43:34.407 --> 00:43:39.377
Texas and into Mexico because the schools
are old enough to have established some proof.

00:43:39.377 --> 00:43:45.087
There will be proof of it, I predict, in 3
years in Naples, Florida, that any bunch of

00:43:45.087 --> 00:43:49.527
idiots can start a charter school if they've
got the right idea about it and they work

00:43:49.527 --> 00:43:56.527
hard and they can get spectacular results.
Why don't we have the freedom to do that?

00:43:58.427 --> 00:44:05.427
Why is the bias not in favor of that? Why
is the college and why are people who start

00:44:07.197 --> 00:44:14.197
these charter schools held up to public ridicule
and suspicion about all this? Why did it happen

00:44:15.047 --> 00:44:20.797
in Bloomington, Indiana, not long ago, that
somebody said that Hillsdale College was not

00:44:20.797 --> 00:44:27.797
fit to be involved in such a thing because
it was too conservative.

00:44:28.287 --> 00:44:35.287
What is the thing that it wishes to conserve?
The beauty of human equality and the rule

00:44:36.207 --> 00:44:43.207
of law and the culture and the arts that flow
from those things. If that's being bigoted,

00:44:43.467 --> 00:44:50.467
then I very much am that.
Tucker: Tucker Anderson. I'm glad I finally

00:44:52.357 --> 00:44:59.357
got to one of these after 25 years. Got to
see my friend Larry again. 2 related observations,

00:45:08.267 --> 00:45:15.267
which I would like your comment on. First,
none of these are going to be pretty.

00:45:15.607 --> 00:45:22.607
First, in fact, in several very important
ways, the ability of us to conduct the fight

00:45:22.897 --> 00:45:28.077
in which we're engaged should be made considerably
easier by the interna- and by the thirst for

00:45:28.077 --> 00:45:34.547
knowledge worldwide and not just the battle
in our country, but the battle on the worldwide

00:45:34.547 --> 00:45:41.547
basis.
Second, in that regard, that it seems to be

00:45:43.097 --> 00:45:49.717
very interesting how individuals elsewhere,
who have not had the privileges that we have

00:45:49.717 --> 00:45:56.717
in a constitutional democracy, are so hungry
and interested in ideas which are being rejected

00:46:01.017 --> 00:46:05.527
here.
Larry: Thank you. That's Tucker Anderson,

00:46:05.527 --> 00:46:12.527
who has been a friend of mine for probably
more than 25 years, and a great guy. New Yorker.

00:46:15.657 --> 00:46:20.787
The first thing about the internet. When I
first came to Hillsdale College a long time

00:46:20.787 --> 00:46:26.057
ago, people would say, "Gosh, it's a really
great college, but what difference can it

00:46:26.057 --> 00:46:29.857
possibly make? It's just a puny little place
in the middle of nowhere." I used to get that

00:46:29.857 --> 00:46:36.857
argument all the time. I repudiate that argument
because learning has a way. The taking over

00:46:41.457 --> 00:46:48.397
... A negative and a positive example.
Positive example, the schools were the leaders

00:46:48.397 --> 00:46:55.397
of the American Revolution were instructed,
number about 10. The number of those key leaders

00:46:56.617 --> 00:47:03.137
who were crucial to the job number about 150.
Others were important, but those guys, take

00:47:03.137 --> 00:47:10.137
them away, different deal. Large numbers matter,
but also small numbers matter.

00:47:10.917 --> 00:47:17.917
Negative example, progressivism came into
America from German historicism through Princeton

00:47:18.227 --> 00:47:23.987
University and Cornell University in the main.
The numbers of people who began were like

00:47:23.987 --> 00:47:30.987
10 and 12. Then it grew from place to place.
I repudiate the thinking that a small place

00:47:32.377 --> 00:47:37.907
can't make a difference, but I don't have
to repudiate by that argument alone any more.

00:47:37.907 --> 00:47:44.907
Hillsdale College has 650 or 700,000 people
who have taken online courses from it. How

00:47:45.197 --> 00:47:52.197
many in this room? Lots. I get stopped in
airports now. It's a marvel to me. I can't

00:47:53.197 --> 00:48:00.197
figure it out because class is really fun,
but since I get a lot of class in my life,

00:48:00.797 --> 00:48:06.907
it wouldn't occur to me to watch a class on
a computer screen, but more than a million

00:48:06.907 --> 00:48:13.907
times ... We think the upward potential of
that is in the millions, and it's still growing.

00:48:14.337 --> 00:48:21.337
There's that and I'll hasten to add something.
It matters that Tucker and I have known each

00:48:23.617 --> 00:48:30.127
other for a long time. It matters that we've
sat together many times and had a good talk.

00:48:30.127 --> 00:48:34.577
I know his ways. I know he's proud of where
he went to school, all that stuff, although

00:48:34.577 --> 00:48:41.577
it's too liberal for him. I know all that
stuff. Things like that matter. I'm married

00:48:45.967 --> 00:48:52.967
to Penny, and we're not married on the internet.
We have a thing going on.

00:48:55.457 --> 00:49:02.457
A classroom is a tremendous place. I want
you all to come because you should see what

00:49:05.477 --> 00:49:10.936
a college is like when it's still like what
a college is like. One of the best things

00:49:10.936 --> 00:49:15.936
I do in my life, apart from teach, is I eat
in the dining hall with the students. I sit

00:49:15.936 --> 00:49:20.507
across from them and we're eating. We're in
our dining hall, and they know I'm going to

00:49:20.507 --> 00:49:25.677
torture them. Also they tell me things, and
I learn things.

00:49:25.677 --> 00:49:32.677
That means that a few people can do things
that very many cannot, but it's also proven

00:49:33.637 --> 00:49:40.417
true, Tucker, that if somebody is really good
at doing things that a few people can do,

00:49:40.417 --> 00:49:47.417
that can radiate everywhere and is doing.
There's great hope in that. I agree. That's

00:49:49.857 --> 00:49:56.857
why it ticks me off so much that they took
a dramatic and lawless step to socialize the

00:49:58.527 --> 00:50:04.947
internet last week. Let me just explain what
the step is.

00:50:04.947 --> 00:50:10.857
We have built the fourth branch of government,
which is the regulatory state. It's much the

00:50:10.857 --> 00:50:17.617
largest part of the government. The theory
that justifies it, if there is such a theory,

00:50:17.617 --> 00:50:24.617
is that Congress and the president delegate
both legislation and enforcement to agencies

00:50:26.307 --> 00:50:33.307
that specialize in particular areas. Those
agencies also enforce the rules they make,

00:50:35.377 --> 00:50:42.377
hear disputes with their employees of enforcement
actions. They're the legislative executive

00:50:42.497 --> 00:50:48.186
in judicial branches.
That's all legitimate because they're ultimately

00:50:48.186 --> 00:50:55.186
under the control of the regular legislative
process and because no one part of the popular

00:50:55.407 --> 00:51:02.407
branches, neither the Congress nor the president,
controls them. The number of people who work

00:51:04.347 --> 00:51:11.347
in the state and federal is about 20 million
people. The idea is all divided up and there

00:51:12.757 --> 00:51:19.757
are new kinds of checks and balances, but
what's not fully understood is it represents

00:51:20.817 --> 00:51:27.817
a kind of government, a different way of doing
it, justified by different principles.

00:51:28.047 --> 00:51:35.047
Our government, by consent, is justified by
the fact that we can talk to each other. We

00:51:35.697 --> 00:51:42.697
have human souls, which is a reflection of
the divine soul. This kind of government is

00:51:42.977 --> 00:51:49.977
justified by expertise and specialization,
the one who knows. Who should decide whether

00:51:51.287 --> 00:51:57.936
20% of the income of the American people is
spent to relieve climate change? Should the

00:51:57.936 --> 00:52:02.467
American people make that decision or should
experts make that decision?

00:52:02.467 --> 00:52:09.467
It's an FCC thing. I read in Wall Street Journal,
the most powerful lobbyist in America, these

00:52:12.007 --> 00:52:16.917
big telecom and technology companies, thought
they had this thing so that they weren't going

00:52:16.917 --> 00:52:22.277
to do net neutrality, which is, by the way,
just stopping people from selling something

00:52:22.277 --> 00:52:29.277
to the highest bidder. With one speech, Obama
turned it around and it's going to be done

00:52:33.186 --> 00:52:38.947
in a month.
We know that Obama employed a man, who is

00:52:38.947 --> 00:52:44.517
a law professor at the University of Chicago
and has been on the cover of GQ. My, is he

00:52:44.517 --> 00:52:51.517
not pretty? Cass Sunstein is his name. He
says that the federal government should have

00:52:53.547 --> 00:52:59.047
proper diversity on their websites, on the
internet. Is that what's going to happen?

00:52:59.047 --> 00:53:06.047
The fairness doctrine for the internet? The
principles are laid down. Thank you Tucker.

00:53:10.877 --> 00:53:17.877
Anybody else? Thanks. Oh wait. One more.
Speaker 5: It's a pleasure to have heard you.

00:53:28.487 --> 00:53:35.487
My friend Biil Lomoth of 50 years has talked
a lot about you. I'd like to ask you a question

00:53:36.047 --> 00:53:43.047
regarding the rule of law. When it was obvious
the Soviet Union was going to break up, there

00:53:44.047 --> 00:53:51.047
would be 15 countries, places like Uzbekistan
and Pakistan and all those, which would want

00:53:52.357 --> 00:53:58.847
to become democracies. For them to become
democracies, they take that sign on the wall

00:53:58.847 --> 00:54:04.887
which said, "General Secretary of the Communist
Party, Uzbekistan," and turn it around. He'd

00:54:04.887 --> 00:54:11.157
say, "Democratically elected President of
the Republic of Uzbekistan."

00:54:11.157 --> 00:54:18.157
I began a conference. We'll have the 18th
one this year. We've had 1,400 chief justices

00:54:22.057 --> 00:54:25.817
come and attend my conferences from 108 countries.
I ran into a problem. I was always able to

00:54:25.817 --> 00:54:32.197
distinguish the rule of man and the rule of
law. The rule of man is you run an election,

00:54:32.197 --> 00:54:38.037
you lose, you put 10,000 people on the street
and the try to burn down the building. That's

00:54:38.037 --> 00:54:44.607
rule of man. Rule of law is you lose the election,
and you go to the court. The court reviews

00:54:44.607 --> 00:54:47.897
the election and say, "Well, you're quite
right."

00:54:47.897 --> 00:54:54.897
Now I run to a third option, the rule of Sharia.
I wonder if you could comment for us, who

00:54:58.537 --> 00:55:02.857
are devoted to the rule of law, on how we
deal with the rule of Sharia, which is when

00:55:02.857 --> 00:55:09.857
the supreme court of a country decides a case,
somebody appeals it to the Sharia court and

00:55:11.627 --> 00:55:16.936
the Sharia court decides differently from
the court. Thank you.

00:55:16.936 --> 00:55:23.936
Larry: Did you say Sharia court?
Speaker 5: Sharia law. There are Sharia courts,

00:55:25.237 --> 00:55:27.317
as you know.
Larry: Yeah.

00:55:27.317 --> 00:55:34.317
Speaker 5: The first comment I'll make is
if you've entertained 1,400 chief justices

00:55:35.737 --> 00:55:42.737
from 180 countries, you, sir, have too many
chiefs and not enough Indians. The second

00:55:45.617 --> 00:55:52.617
thing I'll say is the rule of law is a simple
and beautiful conception and a direction derivation

00:55:57.397 --> 00:56:02.787
from the nature of the human being. All it
means is that the rules are general in their

00:56:02.787 --> 00:56:07.467
nature so everyone can understand them.
There are just these steps. They have to be

00:56:07.467 --> 00:56:12.787
made in advance so you don't know who is going
to be affected by them. Then they have to

00:56:12.787 --> 00:56:19.787
be applied the same, generally the same in
all cases. That's a big order. That's very

00:56:19.917 --> 00:56:26.917
hard to get that because where the rubber
meets the road is when you show up in court

00:56:31.617 --> 00:56:37.987
and somebody knows Obama doesn't like you.
There's a whole bunch of people who are standing

00:56:37.987 --> 00:56:44.987
outside calling you a racist.
The media is ready to repeat the charge and

00:56:49.867 --> 00:56:56.867
waiting breathlessly for the verdict. The
attorney general of the United States has

00:56:58.627 --> 00:57:04.257
threatened that if you're acquitted, you'll
be arrested immediately on a state charge,

00:57:04.257 --> 00:57:11.257
on a federal charge. The rule of law means
none of that makes a blind bit of difference.

00:57:18.857 --> 00:57:25.857
That's a hard thing to achieve. Nothing is
so precious.

00:57:27.377 --> 00:57:32.087
The constitution of the United States is the
greatest protector of the rule of law in human

00:57:32.087 --> 00:57:39.087
history and it is under assault. Everyone
must fear. One of my favorite things to say

00:57:39.587 --> 00:57:44.317
in the last 3 years ... I said it in a board
meeting yesterday ... Is we have to remember

00:57:44.317 --> 00:57:50.897
that we don't enjoy the protections of the
law fully any more. Now we have public opinion.

00:57:50.897 --> 00:57:57.897
We have the good order of the college, the
excellence of its operations.

00:57:59.686 --> 00:58:04.867
We have the fact that we don't take any money
from the government, which is a moral fact.

00:58:04.867 --> 00:58:10.347
It used to be a legal fact. It used to protect
you because they couldn't regulate you unless

00:58:10.347 --> 00:58:17.347
you took their money. Those are powerful things
and we rely on them, but they're not as powerful

00:58:18.497 --> 00:58:24.727
as the rule of law. We fear and we wish to
restore the rule of law.

00:58:24.727 --> 00:58:31.727
About the Sharia courts, it's a simple thing
to understand what's going on in the world.

00:58:31.877 --> 00:58:35.877
What Larry Kudlow said was very artful last
night. He said we should refer to them as

00:58:35.877 --> 00:58:42.877
totalitarian countries, but our country - and
by the way, it just so happens, Hillsdale

00:58:42.947 --> 00:58:49.947
College - both are founded on the principle
of civil and religious freedom.

00:58:50.107 --> 00:58:56.587
The reason the first amendment is the most
important of the amendments is that's the

00:58:56.587 --> 00:59:03.587
one that protects the specifically human activities.
The second amendment is important. It has

00:59:03.607 --> 00:59:10.607
to do with self defense and defense of the
nation, but you know a wolf can protect itself.

00:59:11.247 --> 00:59:18.247
It just can't talk about it. It can't worship
its God, doesn't know what it is or what God

00:59:19.297 --> 00:59:26.297
is.
That regime is beautiful because it recognizes

00:59:31.787 --> 00:59:38.787
something essential in the human being. Also,
by the way, it makes sense under the dispensations

00:59:38.817 --> 00:59:45.817
of both Christianity and Judaism. The reason
for that is Judaism is a universal religion

00:59:49.237 --> 00:59:55.637
and a political religion, but it's not universally
political.

00:59:55.637 --> 01:00:02.637
Only the poor Jews got chosen, and that's
a hard duty. Read the story of the Old Testament

01:00:03.176 --> 01:00:10.176
and find out whether it's really a great privilege
to be the chosen people. They're given a law,

01:00:11.517 --> 01:00:18.417
and only they are given that law. That means
that over them that law comes from above and

01:00:18.417 --> 01:00:24.977
outside the political system. You can't appeal
against it. When God speaks, that's it, and

01:00:24.977 --> 01:00:29.607
he does speak.
Christianity is different. It's a universal

01:00:29.607 --> 01:00:34.967
religion, but not in any sense a political
religion. My kingdom is not of this world.

01:00:34.967 --> 01:00:40.186
It tells you all kinds of rules of behavior.
It doesn't give any laws, and it doesn't appoint

01:00:40.186 --> 01:00:47.186
any political bodies, no magistrates, no legislators,
no executors. That's one of the reasons why

01:00:47.297 --> 01:00:50.407
Jesus was killed, by the way, because they're
impatient about that. They think they're going

01:00:50.407 --> 01:00:57.407
to get a conqueror. He was not.
I'm no expert on Islam. I just happen to be

01:01:00.627 --> 01:01:07.627
a fellow who can see. I know that, like Moses,
Mohammed was the general and a conqueror and

01:01:14.387 --> 01:01:21.387
a ruler. I know that there is a law. I know
that this law coming directly from Mohammed

01:01:24.587 --> 01:01:31.587
and therefore from God is said to be sovereign
universe.

01:01:33.227 --> 01:01:40.227
That places the people who interpret it in
charge. If the Mullahs want a thing in many

01:01:42.587 --> 01:01:49.587
countries, that is the thing that happens.
To appeal against it is to disobey God. That

01:01:51.926 --> 01:01:58.926
means that that regime and the regime of civil
and religious liberty are directly opposite

01:02:00.347 --> 01:02:07.347
regimes. That looks like then that there's
got to be a choice made. Like in so many areas

01:02:12.436 --> 01:02:19.436
today, everything hangs on the choice.
Bill Lomoth has been a friend of mine for

01:02:19.817 --> 01:02:22.917
15 years and I don't know a better man. Thanks.

